{{refbegin}}
*{{note|1|1}} [[Pharyngealization|Emphatic]] consonants are not commonly pronounced by illiterate speakers, which suggests that they are foreign and borrowed from Literary Arabic.
*{{note|2|2}} Many Egyptians cannot pronounce <span style="background:#FFC9CF;padding:2px 2px;">{{IPA|[p, v, ʒ]}}</span>, which are mostly found in names or loanwords from languages like English, French and Persian, not Literary Arabic.{{sfnp|Watson|2002|p=22}}
*: {{IPAblink|ʒ}}, which can come from the deaffrication of foreign {{IPAslink|d͡ʒ}} in Egyptianized loanwords, tends to merge with {{IPAblink|ʃ}}. For example, {{lang|arz|جراش}} ('garage') is mostly pronounced {{IPA|[ɡɑˈɾɑːʃ]}}, even by educated speakers.{{citation needed|date=July 2015}}
* {{note|3|3}} A few [[rural]] speakers away from [[Cairo]] pronounce this {{IPAblink|ʒ}} instead of {{IPAblink|ɡ}},{{citation needed|date=July 2015}} but doing so in native vocabulary is not considered [[Prestige (sociolinguistics)|prestigious]].
* {{note|4|4}} {{Harvcoltxt|Watson|2002}} argues that emphatic <span style="background:#CAFF70;padding:2px 2px;">{{IPA|/ɾˤ, bˤ, mˤ, lˤ/}}</span> are additional consonants in Egyptian Arabic with marginal status.
* {{note|5|5}} <span style="background:#EEE8AA;padding:2px 2px;">{{IPA|/zˤ, q/}}</span> appear in borrowings from Literary Arabic with {{IPA|/ðˤ, q/}}. In inherited words, the two phonemes regularly became {{IPA|/dˤ, ʔ/}} respectively.
*: Non-Egyptianized loanwords with {{IPAslink|q}} may either be Egyptianized to {{IPAblink|ʔ}} or approximated to {{IPAblink|k}}, with the front vowel {{IPAslink|æ}} being backed to {{IPAblink|ɑ}} in a word having an open vowel in the latter case.
*: {{IPA|/ðˤ/}} in borrowings from Literary Arabic are realized as {{IPA|/zˤ/}}. A few words with original {{IPA|/dˤ/}} became with {{IPA|/zˤ/}}, e.g. from the [[triliteral]] root ض-ب-ط as in {{lang|ar|ضابط}} turned to {{lang|arz|ظابط}} {{IPA|[ˈzˤɑːbetˤ]}}, "police/military officer".
*: Non-Egyptianized loanwords having [[interdental consonant]]s ({{IPAslink|θ}}, {{IPAslink|ð}}) are approximated to the [[sibilant consonant|sibilant]]s {{IPAblink|s}}, {{IPAblink|z}}.
{{refend}}

Traditionally, the <!--the case isn't restricted to words of Literary Arabic origin--> [[interdental consonant]]s {{IPA|/θ ð ðˤ/}} correspond to the Egyptian Arabic [[alveolar consonant]]s {{IPA|/t d dˤ/}}.{{citation needed|date=July 2015}} This is a feature common to some North African Arabic varieties and is attested in pre-modern, inherited words:
* {{IPA|/θ/}} > {{IPA|/t/}}: {{IPA|/taʕlab/}} ('fox') from {{IPA|*/θaʕlab/}} {{lang|ar|ثعلب}} (never *{{IPA|/saʕlab/}}). Likewise: {{IPA|/talɡ/}} ('ice') from {{IPA|*/θalɟ/}} {{lang|ar|ثلج}}, {{IPA|/taman/}} ('price') from {{IPA|*/θaman/}} {{lang|ar|ثمن}}, {{IPA|/talaːta/}} ('three') from {{IPA|*/θalaːθa/}} {{lang|ar|ثلاثة}}, {{IPA|/miħraːt/}} ('plough') from {{IPA|*/miħraːθ/}} {{lang|ar|محراث}}, and {{IPA|/ʕatar/}} ('tripped') from *{{IPA|/ʕaθar/}} {{lang|ar|عثر}}.
* {{IPA|/ð/}} > {{IPA|/d/}}: {{IPA|/deːl/}} ('tail') from {{IPA|*/ðajl/}} {{lang|ar|ذيل}} and never {{IPA|/zajl/}}. Likewise {{IPA|/dakar/}} ('male') from {{IPA|*/ðakar/}} {{lang|ar|ذكر}}, {{IPA|/kidib/}} ('lied') from {{IPA|*/kaðib/}} {{lang|ar|كذب}}, and {{IPA|/diːb/}} ('wolf') from {{IPA|*/ðiʔb/}} {{lang|ar|ذئب}}.
* {{IPA|/ðˤ/}} > {{IPA|/d(ˤ)/}}: {{IPA|/d(ˤ)ufr/}} ('nail') from {{IPA|*/ðˤufr/}} {{lang|ar|ظفر}} (never *{{IPA|/zˤufr/}}). Likewise {{IPA|/ðˤ/}} > {{IPA|/dˤ/}}: {{IPA|/dˤalma/}} ('darkness'), from {{IPA|*/ðˤulma/}} {{lang|ar|ظلمة}}; {{IPA|/ʕadˤm/}} ('bone'), from عظم {{IPA|/ʕaðˤm/}}.
However, unlike other North African varieties, in Egyptian Arabic, the Literary Arabic interdental consonants {{IPA|/θ ð ðˤ/}} may correspond to [[sibilant consonant]]s {{IPA|/s z zˤ/}}, particularly in more recent learned borrowings.{{sfnp|Watson|2002|p=22}}

* {{IPA|/θ/}} > {{IPA|/s/}}: {{IPA|/sawra/}} ('revolution'), from Literary {{IPA|/θawra/}} {{lang|ar|ثورة}}
* {{IPA|/ð/}} > {{IPA|/z/}}: {{IPA|/ʔizaːʕa/}} ('broadcasting'), from Literary {{IPA|/ʔiðaːʕa/}} {{lang|ar|إذاعة}}
* {{IPA|/ðˤ/}} > {{IPA|/zˤ/}}: {{IPA|/bazˤr/}} ('clitoris'), from Literary {{IPA|/baðˤr/}} {{lang|ar|بظر}}

The correspondent phoneme of the Classical Arabic ''{{Transliteration|ar|ALA-LC|j}}'', {{lang|ar|ج}} {{IPA|*/ɟ/}}, is realized as a [[velar consonant|velar]] in the dialect of Cairo, in the same way as it is in some Arabic dialects of southern Yemen.{{sfnp|Watson|2002|p=16}} Thus, ''{{Transliteration|ar|DIN|ǧabal}}'' {{lang|ar|جبل}} ('mountain') is pronounced, even in Literary Arabic, as {{IPA|[ˈɡæbæl]}} rather than {{IPA|/d͡ʒabal/}}.

The linguist Janet C. E. Watson considers the following to be additional marginal consonants:{{sfnp|Watson|2002|p=22}}
* The emphatic rhotic {{IPA|/rˤ/}} in some native words, such as {{IPA|[ˈbɑʔɑɾi]}} ('my cows', {{IPA|/ˈbaʔarˤ/}} being 'cows'), which contrasts with {{IPA|[ˈbæʔæɾi]}} ('cow-like, of cows', a derived adjective). Additionally, in loanwords from European languages, such as {{IPA|[bɑɾɑˈʃot]}} ('parachute').
* The labial emphatics {{IPA|/bˤ/}} and {{IPA|/mˤ/}} also in loanwords; minimal pairs include {{IPA|/bˤaːbˤa/ [ˈbɑːbɑ]}} ('patriarch') vs {{IPA|/baːba/ [ˈbæːbæ]}} ('[[Paopi]]').

Classical Arabic *{{IPAslink|q}} became {{IPAblink|ʔ}} in Cairo and the Nile Delta (a feature also shared with [[Levantine Arabic]]),{{sfnp|Watson|2002|p=17}} but {{IPAslink|q}} is retained natively in some dialects to the west of the Nile Delta, outside of [[Alexandria]],{{sfnp|Behnstedt|Woidich|1985|p=?}} and has been reintroduced as a marginal phoneme from Standard Arabic, particularly relating to certain religious words,{{sfnp|Watson|2002|p=17}} besides others such as those deriving from the root {{IPA|/θ-q-f/}}, relating to the [[intellect]] and [[culture]]. {{IPA|/q/}} may be used to distinguish between homophones, at least in mildly careful speech. For example, {{lang|ar|قانون}} {{IPA|/ʔæˈnuːn/}} may be disambiguated as {{IPA|[qɑˈnuːn]}} ('law') vs. {{IPA|[ʔæˈnuːn]}} ('[[kanun (instrument)|kanun]], a musical instrument'); {{lang|ar|قوى}} {{IPA|/ˈʔæwi/}} as {{IPA|[ˈqɑwi]}} ('strong') or the colloquial adverb {{IPA|[ˈʔæwi]}} ('very').<ref name="أبونا_داوود">{{Citation |title=ربنا قوي أوي - تأمل - أبونا داوود لمعي |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3vKGdMhNSDU |access-date=2023-12-23 |language=arz}}</ref> {{IPAslink|v}},<ref>{{Citation |title=الحلقة 11 نيفين الجندي {{!}} ازاي تلاقي نفسك |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=w0JHMyGkcf8&t=67s |access-date=2023-12-23 |language=arz}}</ref> {{IPAslink|p}},<ref>{{Citation |title=د. پيتر حبشي كل ما تريد معرفته عن جفاف العين. |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CaVVjV9r05s&t=467s |access-date=2023-12-23 |language=arz}}</ref> and {{IPAslink|ʒ}} appear in loanwords such as {{IPA|[ʒæˈkettæ, ˈʒæ(ː)ket]}} ('jacket').<ref>{{Citation |title=نــاقـص حـتـه / وجـيـه عـزيـز ...... شعر على سلامه |url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FnKvRMDvh_c&t=55s |access-date=2023-12-23 |language=arz}}</ref>

==== Allophones ====
===== Assimilation =====
* [[Pharyngeal consonant]]s before {{IPAslink|h}}:
** The sequence {{IPA|/ħ'''h'''/}} is more commonly pronounced {{IPA|[ħ'''ħ''']}}, especially outside of careful speech.{{citation needed|date=July 2015}} For example, {{IPA|/fæˈtæħ'''h'''æ/}} ('opened it<sup>(feminine)</sup>') → {{IPA|[fæˈtæħ'''ħ'''æ]}}.
** The sequence {{IPA|/ʕh/}} is more commonly pronounced {{IPA|[ʕ̞ħ̞]}} (or sometimes {{IPA|[ħħ]}}), especially outside of careful speech.{{citation needed|date=July 2015}} For example, {{IPA|/biˈtaʕha/}} ('hers') → {{IPA|[beˈtæʕ̞ħ̞æ]}} or {{IPA|[beˈtæħħæ]}}.
* {{IPAslink|n}} before {{IPAslink|b}}:
** Often, the sequence of {{IPA|/nb/}} assimilates to {{IPA|[mb]}}. 
**: Examples: {{IPA|/zanb/}} ('guilt') → {{IPA|[zæmb]}}; {{IPA|/ʔanˈbuːba/}} ('tube') → {{IPA|[ʔæmˈbuːbæ]}}; {{IPA|/ilˈlinbi/}} ('[[Edmund Allenby, 1st Viscount Allenby|Allenby]]') → {{IPA|[elˈlembi]}}.<ref group="note">An Egyptian popular film called, ''El-Lembi'' ({{lang|arz|اللمبى}}), named after [[Edmund Allenby, 1st Viscount Allenby|Allenby]], but has no relation to him, it is a light-comedy movie character.</ref>
* [[Sibilant consonant]]s before {{IPAslink|ʃ}}:
** Word-finally:
*** The sequences {{IPA|/sʃ/}} and {{IPA|/sˤʃ/}} are more commonly pronounced {{IPA|[ss]}}, especially outside of careful speech.{{citation needed|date=July 2015}} For example, {{IPA|/mabas'''ʃ'''/}} ('didn't kiss') → {{IPA|[mæˈbæ'''s(s)''']}}
*** The sequences {{IPA|/zʃ/}} and {{IPA|/zˤʃ/}} are more commonly pronounced {{IPA|[ss]}}.{{citation needed|date=July 2015}} For example, {{IPA|/mabazˤʃ/}} ('wasn't corrupt') → {{IPA|[mɑˈbɑs(s)]}}.
** Intervocalic:
*** {{IPA|/sʃ/}} → {{IPA|[ʃʃ]}}: {{IPA|/kiːsʃibsi/}} ('''[[Potato chips|Chipsy]]'' sac') → {{IPA|[ˈkiːʃˈʃibsi]}}

===== Voicing and devoicing =====
For some speakers, there is a [[Consonant voicing and devoicing|voicing and devoicing]] [[Assimilation (linguistics)|assimilation]] for the following consonants:
* The sequence {{IPA|/ʒʃ/}} is more commonly pronounced {{IPA|[ʃʃ]}}. For example, {{IPA|/mamantiʒʃ/}}  ('didn't montage') → {{IPA|[mæmænˈteʃ(ʃ)]}}.
<!--don't add tˁ and dˁ, the latter is a learned consonant -->
* Voiced: {{IPAslink|t}} → {{IPAblink|d}}; {{IPAslink|s}} → {{IPAblink|z}}; {{IPAslink|sˤ}} → {{IPAblink|zˤ}}; {{IPAslink|k}} → {{IPAblink|ɡ}}; {{IPAslink|x}} → {{IPAblink|ɣ}}; {{IPAslink|ʃ}} → {{IPAblink|ʒ}}; {{IPAslink|f}} → {{IPAblink|v}}.
* Devoiced: {{IPAslink|d}} → {{IPAblink|t}}; {{IPAslink|z}} → {{IPAblink|s}}; {{IPAslink|zˤ}} → {{IPAblink|sˤ}}; {{IPAslink|ɡ}} → {{IPAblink|k}}; {{IPAslink|ɣ}} → {{IPAblink|x}}; {{IPAslink|ʒ}} → {{IPAblink|ʃ}}; {{IPAslink|v}} → {{IPAblink|f}}.
When the input consonants are plosives differing only in voicing, the resultant assimilation will be complete.
* Examples on voicing assimilation: {{IPA|/la'''x'''batˤ/}} ('he/it confused') → {{IPA|[ˈlɑ'''ɣ'''bɑtˤ]}}; {{IPA|/ji'''s'''baʔ/}} ('outrun') → {{IPA|[ˈje'''z'''bæʔ]}}; {{IPA|/ma'''ʃ'''ˈbuːh/}} ('suspected') → {{IPA|[mæ'''ʒ'''ˈbuːh]}}; {{IPA|/la'''f'''zˤ/}} ('utter') → {{IPA|[lɑ'''v'''zˤ]}}; {{IPA|/mi'''t'''ˈdaːjiʔ/}} ('annoyed') → {{IPA|[me'''d'''ˈdæːjeʔ]}}, a complete assimilation.
* Examples of devoicing assimilation: {{IPA|/mo'''ɡ'''ˈtamaʕ/}} ('society') → {{IPA|[mo'''k'''ˈtæmæʕ]}}; {{IPA|/jista'''ɣ'''faɾ/}} ('ask forgiveness [of God]') → {{IPA|[jesˈtɑ'''x'''fɑɾ]}}; {{IPA|/xa'''d'''t/}} ('took') → {{IPA|[xæ'''t'''(t)]}}, a complete assimilation.

=== Vowels ===
{{see also|Coptic language#Vowels}}

