The short vowels {{IPA|/ɪ/}} and {{IPA|/ʊ/}} are realized as {{IPAblink|i}} and {{IPAblink|ʊ}} respectively at the end of a word. When {{IPA|/ɪ/}} surfaces as an epenthetic vowel at the end of a word, it is strictly {{IPAblink|e}} contrasting with the [[possessive suffix]] {{IPAblink|i}}. The vowel {{IPAblink|ɑ}} is mostly from non-Semitic words if not in words with [[emphatic consonant]]s.

The symbols {{angbr IPA|e}} and {{angbr IPA|o}} represent vowels that vary between close-mid {{IPA|[{{IPAplink|e}}, {{IPAplink|o}}]}} and near-close {{IPA|[{{IPAplink|e̝}}, {{IPAplink|o̝}}]}}. Their centralized allophones (transcribed with {{angbr IPA|ɘ}} and {{angbr IPA|ɵ}}) have the same variable height: {{IPA|[{{IPAplink|ɘ}} ~ {{IPAplink|ɘ̝}}]}} and {{IPA|[{{IPAplink|ɵ}} ~ {{IPAplink|ɵ̝}}]}}.

The final allophone of {{IPA|/u/}} varies in height between close {{IPAblink|u}} and close-mid {{IPAblink|o}} ({{IPA|[{{IPAplink|ʉ}} ~ {{IPAplink|ɵ}}]}} when centralized). For the sake of simplicity, only {{angbr IPA|u}} and {{angbr IPA|ʉ}} are used in this section.

{|
