{{IPAblink|eː}} and {{IPAblink|oː}} are derived from the [[Classical Arabic]] [[diphthong]]s {{IPA|/aj/}} and {{IPA|/aw/}}, respectively, when occurring in [[closed syllable]]s (i.e. not followed by a vowel).  Note that the diphthongs {{IPA|/aj/}} and {{IPA|/aw/}} also occur in the same environment, due to later deletion of unstressed vowels and resulting contraction, e.g. {{IPA|/mudawla/}} {{IPA|[moˈdæwlæ]}} ('consultation') from Classical {{IPA|*/mudaːwala/}}.{{sfnp|Watson|2002|p=23}} Minimal pairs such as {{IPA|/ʃajla/}} {{IPA|[ˈʃæjlæ]}} ('carrying fem. sg.'  and {{IPA|/ʃeːla/}} {{IPA|[ˈʃeːlæ]}} ('burden') also occur.  Both of these words are derived from {{IPA|*/ʃaːjila/}}; {{IPA|/ʃeːla/}} is the phonologically regular outcome, while {{IPA|/ʃajla/}} is an analogical reformation based on the corresponding participial form {{IPA|/CaCCa/}} of other verbs of the same class.

Egyptian Arabic maintains in all positions the early post-Classical distinctions between short {{IPAslink|i}} and {{IPAslink|u}}. Unlike, for example, [[Levantine Arabic]] dialects, which merge {{IPAslink|i}} and {{IPAslink|u}} into {{IPAblink|e}} in most positions, and [[Moroccan Arabic]], which deletes {{IPAslink|i}}, {{IPAslink|u}} and {{IPAslink|a}} in almost all positions. In particular, note the different shapes and vowel distinctions between {{IPA|[keˈtæːb]}} ('book') and {{IPA|[ɡoˈmæːl]}} ('beautiful' pl.) vs. {{IPA|[ɡeˈmæːl]}} ('camels') and {{IPA|[exˈtɑːɾ]}} ('he chose'); in most Levantine dialects, all the short vowels in these words are [[elision|elided]], leading to the identical shapes {{IPA|/ktaːb/}}, {{IPA|/ʒmaːl/}}, {{IPA|/xtaːr/}}.

;Partial vowel allophones:
The phonemes {{IPA|/a/}} and {{IPA|/aː/}} are in the process of splitting into two phonemes each, resulting in the four Egyptian Arabic phonemes {{IPA|/æ æː ɑ ɑː/}}.  The front and back variants alternate in verbal and nominal paradigms in ways that are largely predictable, but the back variants {{IPA|/ɑ ɑː/}} occur unpredictably in some lexical stems, especially those of non-Semitic origin.  This is discussed more below.

;Vowel allophones:
Vowels {{IPAblink|e}} and {{IPAblink|o}} are often regarded as allophones of the vowels {{IPA|/i/}} and {{IPA|/u/}} respectively instead of constituting separate vowel phonemes; so they cannot form minimal pairs. For further discussion regarding vowel allophony in Egyptian Arabic, see {{harvnb|Georgiou|2018}}.
Also Watson does not consider the short mid vowels {{IPA|[e]}} and {{IPA|[o]}} as phonemes on their own and says that they are not used by most speakers of Cairene.{{sfnp|Watson|2002|p=22|loc=2.4.3 Diphthongs with footnote 6}}
Woidich argues that educated speakers of Cairene when pronouncing carefully and slowly tend to distinguish short {{IPA|[e]}} and {{IPA|[o]}} as the results of shortened {{IPA|/eː/}} and {{IPA|/oː/}} from short {{IPA|[ɪ]}} and {{IPA|[ʊ]}} which leads to minimal pairs between them, but stresses that this does not happen with normal speech tempo.{{sfnp|Woidich|2006|p=7}}

;Epenthesis:
An [[epenthesis|epenthetic]] vowel is automatically inserted after the second of three or more consonants in a cluster to break up such clusters.  The epenthetic vowel is analyzed as {{IPAslink|i}}, even though there is a minimal pair distinguishing in many cases between {{IPAblink|e}} and {{IPAblink|i}}.
*An example contrasting possession:
**{{IPA|/bint ɡa.miː.la/}} {{IPA|[ˈben.t'''e''' ɡæˈmiː.læ]}} ('a beautiful girl', {{lang|arz|بنت جميلة}})
**{{IPA|/bin.t'''i''' ɡa.miː.la/}} {{IPA|[ˈben.t'''i''' ɡæˈmiː.læ]}} ('my girl is beautiful', {{lang|arz|بنتى جميلة}})
*Another example contrasting gender:
**{{IPA|/ʕa.malt ʔeː/}} {{IPA|[ʕæˈmæl.t'''e''' ʔeː]}} ('what did you (m.) do?', {{lang|arz|عملت ايه؟}})
**{{IPA|/ʕa.mal.t'''i''' ʔeː/}} {{IPA|[ʕæˈmæl.t'''i''' ʔeː]}} ('what did you (f.) do?', {{lang|arz|عملتى ايه؟}})

==== Emphasis spreading ====
{{Unreferenced section|date=March 2024}}

Many spoken [[Arabic varieties]] have developed two allophones of the [[Classical Arabic]] vowels {{IPAslink|a}} and {{IPAslink|aː}}, with fronted allophones {{IPA|[æ æː]}} occurring in most circumstances, but backed allophones {{IPA|[ɑ ɑː]}} occurring in the vicinity of [[emphatic consonant]]s.  This process is known as '''emphasis spreading'''. The exact criteria of both "vicinity" and "emphatic consonant" varies depending on the individual speech variety.  In Egyptian Arabic, the occurrence of {{IPA|[ɑ ɑː]}} is no longer completely predictable, suggesting that these sounds have become [[phoneme|phonemicized]] or inherited from the Coptic language, the former language of Egypt; but see below for more discussion.

In Egyptian Arabic, the consonants that trigger emphasis spreading include the [[pharyngealized]] consonants {{IPA|/tˤ dˤ sˤ zˤ/}}, the [[uvular consonant|uvular]] [[stop consonant|stop]] {{IPAslink|q}}, and some instances of {{IPAslink|r}} (see below).  On the other hand, the [[pharyngeal consonant]]s {{IPA|/ħ ʕ/}} do ''not'' trigger emphasis spreading; in the prestigious Cairene dialect, the [[velar consonant|velar]] [[fricative]]s {{IPA|/x ɣ/}} also do not, although this is different in the [[Sa'idi Arabic|Saidi]] variant in which they are [[uvular consonant|uvular]] {{IPA|[χ ʁ]}}.

In general, when emphasis spreading is triggered, the back variants {{IPA|[ɑ ɑː]}} spread both forward and backward throughout the phonological word, including any morphological prefixes, suffixes and [[clitic]]s. Note that this is different from many other Arabic varieties.  For example, in [[Moroccan Arabic]], emphasis spreading usually travels no farther than the first full vowel on either side of the triggering consonant, and in many varieties of [[Levantine Arabic]], emphasis spreading is of indefinite extent but is blocked by the phonemes {{IPA|/j ʃ/}}.  Nevertheless, emphasis spreading is not completely reliable, and there is some free variation, especially in the pronunciation of prefixes and suffixes at some distance from the triggering consonant.

Some instances of {{IPAslink|r}} trigger emphasis spreading, while others do not. Originally, an {{IPAslink|r}} adjacent to {{IPAslink|i}} was considered non-emphatic, while others were emphatic and triggered emphasis spreading.  Currently, however, this is no more than a rough guideline, as many exceptions have since developed.  This situation has led the linguist ''Janet C. E. Watson'', who mostly studied the [[Yemeni Arabic]] dialects, to postulate the existence of two phonemes {{IPA|/r rˤ/}}, which both surface as {{IPA|[r~ɾ]}} but where only {{IPAslink|rˤ}} triggers emphasis spreading.  This analysis is not completely ideal in that these two resulting "phonemes" {{IPA|/r rˤ/}} alternate to a large extent (often unpredictably) in related forms derived from the same root.

Currently, to the extent that the emphatic or non-emphatic variant of {{IPAslink|r}} can be predicted, it works as follows: If {{IPAslink|r}} is adjacent to a vowel {{IPA|/i(ː)/}}, emphasis-spreading is inhibited; otherwise, it occurs.  The {{IPAslink|r}} is able to "see across" [[derivational morphology|derivational]] but not [[inflectional morphology|inflectional]] morphemes.  As an example, {{IPA|[teˈɡɑːɾɑ]}} ('commerce') and {{IPA|[ˈtekbɑɾ]}} ('you grow' masc.) both have emphasis spreading, since {{IPAslink|r}} occurs adjacent to low {{IPA|/a(ː)/}} but not adjacent to any non-low front vowel.  On the other hand, of the derived forms {{IPA|[teˈɡæːɾi]}} ('commercial') and {{IPA|[tekˈbɑɾi]}} ('you grow' fem.), only the latter has emphasis spreading.  In this case, the derivational suffix {{IPA|/-i/}} ('related to') creates a new [[lexical item]] in the language's vocabulary, and hence the stem is reevaluated for emphasis, with the non-low front vowel {{IPA|/i/}} triggering non-emphatic {{IPAslink|r}}; but the inflectional suffix {{IPA|/-i/}} marking feminine singular does not create a new lexical item, and as a result the emphasis in the stem remains. (For these purposes, past and non-past forms of a verb are considered separate stems; hence alternations can occur like {{IPA|/istamarˤrˤ/}} 'he continued' vs. {{IPA|/jistamirr/}} 'he continues'.)

An emphasis-spreading {{IPAslink|r}} is usually adjacent to a low vowel {{IPA|/a(ː)/}} (which in turn is backed to {{IPA|/ɑ(ː)/}}), but that is not necessary, and {{IPA|/u(ː)/}} also triggers emphasis-spreading: Examples {{IPA|/maʃhuːrˤ/}} ('famous') → {{IPA|[mɑʃˈhuːɾ]}}, {{IPA|/maʃrˤuːʕ/}} ('project') → {{IPA|[mɑʃˈruːʕ]}}, {{IPA|/rˤufajjaʕ/}} ('thin') → {{IPA|[roˈfɑjjɑʕ]}}.

The alternation between {{IPAblink|æ|æ(ː)}} and {{IPAblink|ɑ|ɑ(ː)}} is almost completely predictable in verbal and nominal paradigms, as well as in the large majority of words derived from Classical Arabic.  It is also irrelevant for the operation of the numerous phonological adjustment rules (e.g. vowel lengthening, shortening and elision) in Egyptian Arabic.  As a result, linguistic descriptions tend to subsume both under an [[Phoneme#Neutralization and archiphonemes|archiphoneme]] {{IPA|/a(ː)/}}.  On the other hand, there are a number of [[lexical item]]s in which "autonomous" {{IPA|[ɑ ɑː]}} tend to occur irrespective of the presence of emphatic consonants.  A few are in Aramaic-derived words, e.g. {{IPA|[ˈmɑjjɑ]}} ('water'), but the majority are in words of non-Semitic origin — especially those derived from European languages — where {{IPA|[ɑ ɑː]}} echo the vowel quality of {{IPA|/a/}} in those languages.

Different authors have proposed differing phonemic analyses of this situation:
*Some go ahead and treat all occurrences of {{IPA|[æ(ː) ɑ(ː)]}} as separate phonemes, despite the additional complexity of the resulting morphological descriptions;
*Some treat only "autonomous" occurrences of {{IPA|[ɑ(ː)]}} as phonemes {{IPA|/ɑ(ː)/}}, with all the rest subsumed under {{IPA|/a(ː)/}};
*Some have created new emphatic consonants (e.g. analyzing {{IPA|[ˈmɑjjɑ]}} as {{IPA|/mˤajja/}}, where underlying {{IPA|/mˤ/}} surfaces as {{IPAblink|m}} but triggers the back allophone {{IPAblink|ɑ}});
*Some have ignored the distinction entirely.
The approach followed here is to ignore the distinction in phonemic descriptions, subsuming {{IPA|[æ(ː) ɑ(ː)]}} as allophones of {{IPA|/a(ː)/}}, but where necessary to also include a phonetic explication (i.e. detailed pronunciation) that indicates the exact quality of all vowels.  Generally, these phonetic explications are given for the examples in the section on [[#Phonology|phonology]], and elsewhere whenever autonomous {{IPAblink|ɑ|ɑ(ː)}} occurs.

