Because the stress is almost completely predictable, it is not indicated in phonemic transcriptions (but ''is'' given in the corresponding phonetic explication).

== Phonological development ==

=== Phonemic developmental stages ===
{{Harvcoltxt|Omar|Nydell|1973}} identifies three stages in the phonological acquisition of Egyptian Arabic, ending with completion of the consonant inventory (with the possible exception of {{IPAslink|q}}) at approximately age five.{{sfnp|Omar|Nydell|1973|p=52}}

*'''Babbling stage: (~6–10 months)'''
The sound inventory found in the [[babbling]] stage does not technically consist of phonemes, but rather vowel- and consonant-''like'' sounds. Therefore, they are not true speech sounds.{{sfnp|Omar|Nydell|1973|p=35}} Like children acquiring other world languages, Egyptian Arabic infants produce consonant-like sounds approximating {{IPAslink|b}}, {{IPAslink|p}}, {{IPAslink|ʔ}}, {{IPAslink|h}}, {{IPAslink|ɣ}}, {{IPAslink|v}}, {{IPAslink|z}}, {{IPAslink|m}}, {{IPAslink|w}}, {{IPAslink|j}}.{{sfnp|Omar|Nydell|1973|p=38}}

*'''Stage I (~1–2 years)'''
At this stage children have acquired the basic {{IPA|/i/}}, {{IPA|/a/}}, {{IPA|/u/}} vowel triangle, and the consonants {{IPAslink|b}}, {{IPAslink|m}}, {{IPAslink|w}}, {{IPAslink|j}}, and {{IPAslink|h}}. At this stage {{IPAslink|ʔ}} is only produced word-initially (possibly due to speakers’ tendency to insert a glottal stop on words which begin with a vowel). There is typically no voiced-voiceless contrast and no single-[[Gemination|double]] consonant contrast.{{sfnp|Omar|Nydell|1973|p=45}}

*'''Stage II (~2–3 years)'''
Newly acquired phonemes are: {{IPAslink|t}}, {{IPAslink|d}}, {{IPAslink|k}}, {{IPAslink|ɡ}}, {{IPAslink|n}}, {{IPAslink|f}}, {{IPAslink|s}}, {{IPAslink|z}}, {{IPAslink|l}}, {{IPAslink|x}}, {{IPAslink|ɣ}}, {{IPAslink|æ}}, {{IPAslink|e}}, {{IPAslink|o}}. A voiced-voiceless contrast is now apparent in stops and fricatives. Consonant clusters appear but are unstable, often being omitted or simplified ([[Phonological development|consonant cluster reduction]]). The newly acquired [[Lateral consonant|lateral]] {{IPAslink|l}} is frequently used in place of the [[Alveolar flap|flap]]{{sfnp|Omar|Nydell|1973|pp=48–49}}/[[Alveolar trill|trill]] {{IPAblink|ɾ}}~{{IPAblink|r}} ([[Lateral consonant|lateralization]]). Example: {{IPA|/madɾasa/}} ('school') → {{IPA|[mædˈlæsæ]}}

*'''Intermediate Stage II-III (~3–5 years)'''
Vowel length distinction, the [[Emphatic consonant|emphatics]] {{IPAslink|sˤ}}, {{IPAslink|zˤ}}, {{IPAslink|tˤ}}, {{IPAslink|dˤ}}; &nbsp; {{IPAslink|ʕ}} (sometimes realized as {{IPAslink|ʔ}}) and {{IPAslink|ʃ}} (often realized as {{IPAblink|s}}) are acquired. A [[Gemination|geminated consonant]] distinction is developing, although children have difficulty with {{IPA|/xx/}} and its voiced pair {{IPA|/ɣɣ/}}.{{sfnp|Saleh|Shoeib|Hegazi|Ali|2007|pp=234–240}}

*'''Phonemic Stage III (Adult mastery ~5 years)'''
The flap/trill {{IPAslink|ɾ}}~{{IPAslink|r}} and all diphthongs and clusters are acquired, and geminate consonant distinction is stable. The phoneme {{IPAslink|q}} is rare in Egyptian Arabic and is typically only mastered with formal schooling at around age seven or eight, and is realized acceptably in the dialect as {{IPAblink|k}}.{{sfnp|Omar|Nydell|1973|p=52}}

=== Cross-linguistic comparison and phonological processes ===
Egyptian Arabic phoneme acquisition has been chiefly compared to that of English. The order of phoneme acquisition is similar for both languages: Exceptions are {{IPAslink|s}}, {{IPAslink|z}}, and {{IPAslink|h}}, which appear earlier in Arabic-speaking children's inventory than in English, perhaps due to the frequency of their occurrence in the children's input.{{sfnp|Amayreh|Dyson|1998}} Egyptian Arabic differs most from English in terms of age of phoneme acquisition: Vowel distinctions appear at an earlier age in Egyptian Arabic than in English, which could reflect both the smaller inventory and the higher functional value of Arabic vowels: The consonantal system, on the other hand, is completed almost a year later than that of English.{{sfnp|Omar|Nydell|1973|p=60}} However, the lateral {{IPAslink|l}} is acquired by most Arabic-speaking children by age two, a year earlier than English-speaking children.{{sfnp|Saleh|Shoeib|Hegazi|Ali|2007|pp=154–160}} The most difficult phonemes for young Arabic children are emphatic stops, fricatives, and the flap{{sfnp|Amayreh|Dyson|2000|p=84}}/trill {{IPAslink|ɾ}}~{{IPAslink|r}}. &nbsp; {{IPAslink|x}} and {{IPAslink|ɣ}}, which are relatively rare sounds in other languages, are the most difficult geminate consonants to acquire.{{sfnp|Abou-Elsaad|Baz|El-Banna|2009|pp=275–282}}

==== Phonological processes ====
For children under two, [[Phonological development|syllable reduction]] and [[Phonological development|final consonant deletion]] are the most common phonological processes.{{sfnp|Saleh|Shoeib|Hegazi|Ali|2007|pp=234–240}} De-emphasis, involving the loss of the secondary articulation for emphatic consonants (e.g., realizing emphatic {{IPAslink|sˤ}} as {{IPAblink|s}}), may reflect the motoric difficulty of emphatic consonants, which are rare in world languages,{{sfnp|Amayreh|Dyson|2000|p=94}} as well as their relatively low frequency and functional load in Arabic.{{sfnp|Amayreh|Dyson|2000|p=100}}

The back fricatives {{IPAslink|ħ}} and {{IPAslink|x}} are unusually accurate at an early age and less prone to [[Phonological development|fronting]] than in other languages.{{sfnp|Amayreh|Dyson|2000|p=87}}

=== Acquisition of syllable structure ===
Most children have mastered all syllable types between the ages of two and three. A preference for three-syllable words is evident (CVːC syllables being the most frequently produced) and production rarely exceeds four syllables. Simplification processes like those detailed above may occur to reduce CVCC syllables to CVːC or CVC syllables; however, when children change the syllable structure, they preserve the prosodic weight of the altered syllable in order to maintain stress relations.{{sfnp|Ammar|2002|pp=154–158}}

